# In-App Chat System — Implementation Plan

## Problem & Goal

Currently, "Chat with Lead Generator" opens **WhatsApp** — messages leave the app, there's no history, and no privacy control. The goal is to build a **full in-app chat system** with:

1. **Lead-specific chat** — When a claimer/generator clicks "Chat", it opens a conversation tied to that lead
2. **Dedicated Chat page** (`/messages`) — Left sidebar with all conversations, center panel with chat messages
3. **End-to-end encryption (E2E)** — Messages encrypted before leaving the browser
4. **Real-time messaging** — Via Supabase Realtime (already used in Community)
5. **Persistent storage** — All messages saved in Supabase (encrypted at rest)

---

## Architecture Overview

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph Frontend
        A["LeadDetails Page"] -->|"Chat with Generator"| B["Opens /messages?lead=xyz"]
        C["Chat Page /messages"] -->|Left panel| D["Conversation List"]
        C -->|Center panel| E["Chat Window"]
        E -->|encrypt before send| F["Web Crypto API"]
        E -->|decrypt on receive| F
    end

    subgraph Supabase
        G["conversations table"]
        H["messages table"]
        I["user_keys table"]
        J["Supabase Realtime"]
    end

    F -->|"encrypted ciphertext"| H
    J -->|"INSERT event"| E
    G --- H
```

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **End-to-End Encryption Trade-offs**
> 
> True E2E encryption means the **server cannot read messages**. This has consequences:
> - **No server-side search** — Users can only search messages locally in the browser
> - **Key loss = message loss** — If a user clears browser data, old messages can't be decrypted (we mitigate with key export/backup)
> - **No admin moderation** — Admins can't read chats to resolve disputes
>
> **Recommended approach:** AES-256-GCM via the browser's built-in **Web Crypto API** (zero dependencies). Each conversation gets a unique symmetric key, shared between the two participants using ECDH key exchange.

> [!WARNING]
> **Database Migration Required**
> 
> This feature needs 3 new tables (`conversations`, `messages`, `user_keys`) and RLS policies. You'll need to run the SQL migration in your Supabase dashboard.

---

## Technology & Libraries

| Layer | Technology | Why |
|-------|-----------|-----|
| **Encryption** | Web Crypto API (built-in) | Zero dependencies, AES-256-GCM + ECDH, browser-native |
| **Real-time** | Supabase Realtime | Already used in Community page, zero extra setup |
| **Database** | Supabase Postgres | Existing backend, just add new tables |
| **Key Storage** | IndexedDB (via `idb-keyval`) | ~1KB library, stores private keys securely in browser |
| **Frontend** | React + existing UI components | Consistent with existing `shadcn/ui` design system |

**No heavy libraries needed** — everything uses browser-native APIs + your existing Supabase stack.

---

## Database Schema (3 New Tables)

### 1. `conversations`
```sql
CREATE TABLE conversations (
  id          UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  lead_id     UUID REFERENCES leads(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,  -- optional, links to lead
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  updated_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);
```

### 2. `conversation_participants`
```sql
CREATE TABLE conversation_participants (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  conversation_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES conversations(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  user_id         UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES profiles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  joined_at       TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  last_read_at    TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  UNIQUE(conversation_id, user_id)
);
```

### 3. `messages`
```sql
CREATE TABLE messages (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  conversation_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES conversations(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  sender_id       UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES profiles(id),
  ciphertext      TEXT NOT NULL,      -- E2E encrypted message content
  iv              TEXT NOT NULL,      -- Initialization vector for AES-GCM
  created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);
```

### 4. `user_keys` (Public keys for E2E encryption)
```sql
CREATE TABLE user_keys (
  id          UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  user_id     UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES profiles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE UNIQUE,
  public_key  TEXT NOT NULL,          -- ECDH public key (JWK format)
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);
```

### RLS Policies (Security)
- **conversations / participants**: Users can only see conversations they participate in
- **messages**: Users can read messages from their conversations; can only insert as themselves
- **user_keys**: Public keys are readable by all authenticated users; each user can only write their own

---

## End-to-End Encryption Flow

### How It Works (No Extra Libraries)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant A as User A (Generator)
    participant DB as Supabase (sees only ciphertext)
    participant B as User B (Claimer)
    
    Note over A: First time: generate ECDH key pair
    A->>DB: Store public key in user_keys
    B->>DB: Store public key in user_keys
    
    Note over A: Starting a chat with B
    A->>DB: Fetch B's public key
    Note over A: ECDH: derive shared secret from<br/>A's private key + B's public key
    Note over A: AES-256-GCM encrypt message
    A->>DB: Send { ciphertext, iv }
    
    DB-->>B: Realtime: new message event
    Note over B: ECDH: derive same shared secret from<br/>B's private key + A's public key
    Note over B: AES-256-GCM decrypt ciphertext
    B->>B: Display plaintext message
```

### Key Management
1. **On first login/app load**: Generate ECDH key pair → store private key in **IndexedDB**, upload public key to `user_keys`
2. **On chat open**: Fetch other user's public key → derive shared AES key → encrypt/decrypt
3. **Key backup**: Option to export key as a file (future enhancement)

### Crypto Operations (All via Web Crypto API)
```
Key Generation:   crypto.subtle.generateKey('ECDH', P-256)
Key Derivation:   crypto.subtle.deriveKey(ownPrivate + otherPublic → AES-256-GCM)
Encrypt:          crypto.subtle.encrypt('AES-GCM', sharedKey, plaintext)
Decrypt:          crypto.subtle.decrypt('AES-GCM', sharedKey, ciphertext)
```

